    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d381">

                <pb facs="1100" n="1084" sameAs="frus:frus1951v01/pg1084"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">460.509/6–551</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by the Deputy Assistant
                        Secretary of State for Economic Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Linder</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1951-06-05">June 5, 1951</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Subject: Battle Report and Proposed Legislation<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d381-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>Congressman Battle told me this afternoon that the House Foreign Affairs
                    Committee decided this morning to authorize the Subcommittee to publish their
                    report as a Subcommittee report. He indicated that most of the changes we
                    suggested will be incorporated in the report.</p>
                <p>As to the Committee print, that, too, was submitted, unfortunately as originally
                    drafted, to the full Committee which decided to refer it to the State Department
                    for comment and a report. Battle felt that there was a disposition on the part
                    of the Committee to look into the matter carefully and finally to propose
                    legislation with which we could live.</p>
                <p>I told him that the reference in the President’s letter<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d381-f2"/> expressing the hope that the Congress would enact new
                    legislation in place of the <pb facs="1101" n="1085" xml:id="pg1085"/> Kem
                    Amendment was written with the Battle Committee work in mind.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d381-f1" n="1" xml:id="d381-f1-content">
                <p>For information concerning the Battle Report and the Mutual Defense Assistance
                    Control Act of 1951 (Battle Act), see the editorial note, <ref target="#pg1176"
                        >p. 1176</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d381-f2" n="2" xml:id="d381-f2-content">
                <p>A copy of the President’s letter was not found in Department of State files. The
                    President’s desire for new legislation to replace the Kem Amendment was also
                    expressed in his statement to the press on June 2 when he signed the Third
                    Supplemental Appropriation Act; for the text of this statement, see the
                    Department of State <hi rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, June 25, 1951, pp.
                    1027–1029.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
